WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.052
A loop needs a way to decide whether an action improved the task state.

2
00:00:06.052 --> 00:00:09.943
Without evaluation, the system may produce activity without progress.

3
00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:14.698
It might rewrite the same file several times, generate longer explanations,

4
00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:20.750
or run tools that do not move the task closer to a terminal condition.

5
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.505
Evaluation is the feedback signal that separates useful iteration from noise.

6
00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:27.666
Different tasks need different evaluators.

7
00:00:27.666 --> 00:00:32.421
Deterministic checks include compiler results, schema validation, test suites, policy decisions,

8
00:00:32.421 --> 00:00:35.447
reference comparisons, reconciled totals, and programmatic assertions.

9
00:00:35.447 --> 00:00:41.932
Human review may be necessary when the judgment involves risk, product intent, or business meaning.

10
00:00:41.932 --> 00:00:49.280
Model-based grading can help assess qualities such as clarity or relevance, but it needs a rubric, examples,

11
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.442
calibration, and periodic human review.

12
00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:56.197
A score without a tested rubric is just another generated claim.

13
00:00:56.197 --> 00:00:59.223
Prefer deterministic evidence when it is available.

14
00:00:59.223 --> 00:01:07.004
A passing test, valid configuration, or policy engine result usually gives a clearer signal than asking the same

15
00:01:07.004 --> 00:01:10.030
model whether its own output looks correct.

16
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:16.947
The component that generated an artifact should not be treated as the only authority certifying it.

17
00:01:16.947 --> 00:01:23.863
Separation may come from deterministic checks, different context, an independent evaluator role, or a human reviewer.

18
00:01:23.863 --> 00:01:26.889
Repair should respond to specific failure evidence.

19
00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:32.509
Capture the failed criterion, preserve the relevant observation, form a concrete repair hypothesis,

20
00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:40.290
make the smallest justified change, rerun the relevant evaluation, and compare the new result with the previous state.

21
00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:46.774
If the same failure repeats without new evidence, the loop should stop, narrow the task,

22
00:01:46.774 --> 00:01:49.368
or escalate rather than regenerate randomly.

23
00:01:49.368 --> 00:01:55.852
That stop protects good partial work and prevents uncertainty from being hidden behind more output.

24
00:01:55.852 --> 00:02:01.472
In the coding example, a failing test is not just a red mark.

25
00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:03.201
It is an observation.

26
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:10.118
The next iteration should inspect the related code, propose a specific cause, apply a narrow patch,

27
00:02:10.118 --> 00:02:14.440
and rerun the targeted test before running the full suite.

28
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:21.789
Randomly rewriting the entire feature can destroy working behavior and make cause and effect impossible to trace.

29
00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:27.841
The quality of evaluation determines whether the loop learns, stalls, or becomes falsely confident.
